.docname {Chat}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Conversation | One chat, owned by one account, with its messages inside it | "Why is room 2 hot?", 6 messages |
    | Exchange | One question and its answer, stored together | a user message and an assistant message |
    | History | The latest messages sent to `agent` as context | the last 10 |
    | SSE | Server-Sent Events: one HTTP answer that keeps sending small text frames | `data: {"type":"token","text":"The "}` |
    | Frame | One SSE event, ended by a blank line | a `token` frame |
    | Token | A small piece of the answer, sent as soon as it exists | `"The "` |
    | Citation | A part of a document that the answer is based on | `{"source":"floor-plans.qd", …}` |
    | ObjectId | MongoDB's own id type. It never leaves the storage adapter | shown as `"6650c1…"` |
    | Owner | The account in the `sub` of the claims | `3f2b` |
    | Port | A Rust `trait` the core logic talks to, so tests can swap the real thing for a fake | `ConversationStore`, `AgentClient` |
    | Adapter | The real code behind a port: it talks to MongoDB, to `agent`, or to HTTP | `MongoConversations` |
    | Stream | A value that gives out its items one at a time, as they become ready | the answer, token by token |

`chat` is a Rust service (Axum, MongoDB) in `backend/chat`. It does four things:

1. **Stores conversations.** Each one belongs to one account.
2. **Sends each question to `agent`**, together with the recent history.
3. **Relays the answer to the browser as it is written**, token by token.
4. **Saves the exchange** once the answer is whole.

`agent` stores nothing: every `/ask` stands alone. `chat` is the part that remembers.

Role in the system: [Chat](../architecture/chat-architecture.qd). The other side of the stream: [Agent](agent.qd).

---

## The idea in one minute

Think of a receptionist who takes written questions for an expert in the next room.

- The receptionist keeps a **folder** per visitor, with every question and answer so far.
- A visitor writes a question. The receptionist walks to the expert with the question and the **last few pages** of the folder, so the expert knows what was said before.
- The expert answers **word by word**. The receptionist reads each word out loud to the visitor at once, without waiting for the end.
- Only when the expert says "that is my full answer" does the receptionist **put both pages in the folder**. If the expert stops halfway, nothing is filed.
- A visitor can only ever open their own folder. Another visitor's folder looks like it does not exist.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        B["browser"] -- "POST question" --> C[("chat<br/>the receptionist")]
        C -- "POST /ask + history" --> A["agent<br/>the expert"]
        A -- "tokens, then done" --> C
        C -- "tokens (SSE)" --> B
        C --- DB[("MongoDB<br/>the folders")]

---

## The files

Ports and adapters. `tests/architecture_fitness.rs` fails the build when a rule breaks.

| Path | What it holds | May not import |
|---|---|---|
| `src/domain/conversation.rs` | `Conversation`, `ChatMessage`, `Citation`, the limits, `validate_text`, the history window, the automatic title | axum, mongodb, reqwest, bson, `crate::service`, `crate::adapters` |
| `src/domain/identity.rs` | `GatewayClaims {user_id, raw}` | same |
| `src/service/conversations.rs` | The use cases, and the streaming `Exchange` | any framework, `crate::adapters` |
| `src/service/ports.rs` | `ConversationStore`, `AgentClient`, `AgentEvent` | |
| `src/adapters/driving/http_api/` | The claims extractor, the handlers, the SSE frames, the error mapping | `crate::adapters::driven` |
| `src/adapters/driven/persistence/` | The MongoDB store. The only file that knows `ObjectId` | |
| `src/adapters/driven/agent.rs` | `AgentService`: calls `agent` `/ask` and reads its SSE stream | |
| `src/adapters/ratelimit.rs` | 300 requests per minute per client IP | |

"Driving" means the outside world drives the service (HTTP comes in). "Driven" means the service drives the outside world (it calls MongoDB and `agent`).

Other rules the tests enforce:

- `ObjectId` appears only in `adapters/driven/persistence/conversations.rs`. bson writes it as `{"$oid": "…"}`, which is not what the API returns.
- No module uses a blanket `rename_all = "camelCase"`: citation fields are snake_case and must stay so.
- The claims header name comes only from `claims_schema::CLAIMS_HEADER`.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        H["http_api<br/>controllers"] --> S["Conversations"]
        S --> D["domain<br/>rules"]
        S -. "ConversationStore" .-> M["MongoConversations"]
        S -. "AgentClient" .-> A["AgentService"]
        M --> DB[("chatdb.conversations")]
        A -- "POST /ask, x-gateway-claims" --> Ag["agent"]

---

## `domain/conversation.rs`: the rules, with no framework

### The limits

```rust
pub const MAX_MESSAGES: usize = 100;
pub const MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH: usize = 8000;
pub const MAX_TITLE_LENGTH: usize = 120;
pub const DEFAULT_TITLE: &str = "New chat";
```

A conversation holds at most 100 messages. A question is at most 8000 characters, a title at most 120. A conversation made without a title is called `New chat`.

### The shapes

```rust
pub struct Citation {
    pub chunk_id: String,
    pub document_id: String,
    pub source: String,
    #[serde(default)]
    pub section_path: Option<String>,
}
```

A citation keeps the **Python names** of `agent` (`chunk_id`, not `chunkId`). It is stored and returned unchanged, so a blanket rename to camelCase would silently break every citation. A missing `section_path` is written as `null`.

```rust
pub struct ChatMessage {
    #[serde(rename = "_id")]
    pub id: String,
    pub role: Role,
    pub content: String,
    #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = "Option::is_none")]
    pub citations: Option<Vec<Citation>>,
    #[serde(rename = "createdAt")]
    pub created_at: String,
}
```

One message. `role` is `user` or `assistant`. `skip_serializing_if` means a message with no citations has **no `citations` key at all**. The id and the date are plain strings: the domain never sees `ObjectId` or MongoDB dates.

```rust
pub struct Conversation {
    pub id: String,
    pub user_id: String,
    pub title: String,
    pub messages: Vec<ChatMessage>,
    pub created_at: String,
    pub updated_at: String,
}
```

The full folder. `ConversationSummary` is the same without `messages`, used for the list.

### Checking a text

```rust
pub fn validate_text(
    value: Option<&str>,
    name: &str,
    max_length: usize,
) -> Result<String, DomainError> {
    let text = value
        .map(str::trim)
        .filter(|t| !t.is_empty())
        .ok_or_else(|| DomainError::Validation(format!("{name} must be a non-empty string")))?;

    if text.chars().count() > max_length {
        return Err(DomainError::Validation(format!(
            "{name} cannot exceed {max_length} characters"
        )));
    }
    Ok(text.to_string())
}
```

1. Trim the spaces. Missing, or empty after trimming: `"{name} must be a non-empty string"`.
2. Count the **characters** (not bytes) of the trimmed text. Too long: `"{name} cannot exceed {max} characters"`.
3. Return the trimmed text.

Trimming first matters: trailing spaces never push a valid question over the limit.

**Example.** `"  Why is room 2 hot?  "` becomes `"Why is room 2 hot?"`, 18 characters, fine.

### Three small rules on a conversation

```rust
impl Conversation {
    pub fn has_room_for_exchange(&self) -> bool {
        self.messages.len() + 2 <= MAX_MESSAGES
    }

    pub fn recent_history(&self, limit: usize) -> Vec<HistoryTurn> {
        self.messages
            .iter()
            .skip(self.messages.len().saturating_sub(limit))
            .map(|m| HistoryTurn { role: m.role, content: m.content.clone() })
            .collect()
    }

    pub fn title_from_first_question(&self, question: &str) -> Option<String> {
        if self.title != DEFAULT_TITLE || !self.messages.is_empty() {
            return None;
        }
        Some(question.chars().take(MAX_TITLE_LENGTH).collect())
    }
}
```

- **Room.** One exchange adds **two** messages, so the check is `len + 2 <= 100`. A conversation with 98 messages is allowed one more exchange (it ends at 100). With 100, the next is refused.
- **History.** Skip all but the last `limit` messages, and send only `role` and `content`. With 14 messages and a limit of 10, it skips the first 4 and sends messages 5 to 14. Citations are not sent.
- **Title.** Only the **first** exchange of a conversation still called `New chat` gets a new title: the first 120 characters of the question. Any later exchange leaves the title alone, so a rename is never overwritten.

### The title field has three states

```rust
pub enum TitleField<'a> {
    Absent,
    Present(Option<&'a str>),
}
```

A body without `title` is not the same as a body with `"title": null`. The first means "use the default". The second is an error.

| Body | Result |
|---|---|
| `{}` | Title `New chat` |
| `{"title":"Floor 3"}` | Title `Floor 3`, trimmed |
| `{"title":null}` or `{"title":"  "}` | `400 title must be a non-empty string` |

A typed `Option<String>` field cannot tell "absent" from "null" (both become `None`), so the handler keeps the body as raw JSON and decides itself.

