## `main.rs` and configuration

```rust
fn history_max_messages() -> usize {
    match env::var("HISTORY_MAX_MESSAGES") {
        Err(_) => DEFAULT_HISTORY_MAX_MESSAGES,
        Ok(raw) => raw
            .parse::<usize>()
            .ok()
            .filter(|value| *value >= 1)
            .unwrap_or_else(|| panic!("HISTORY_MAX_MESSAGES must be a positive integer")),
    }
}
```

Read **once at start**. The old Node service re-read and re-checked it on every request, so a bad value only showed up as a `500` on the first message. Now the same mistake is a container that refuses to start. Not set: 10. Set to `0`, to a negative number or to text: panic.

`main` then connects to MongoDB (panic if it cannot), builds `Conversations` from the Mongo store and the `agent` client, builds the router, and serves on `0.0.0.0:{PORT}` with a graceful shutdown on `Ctrl+C` or `SIGTERM`. The connection info is passed (`into_make_service_with_connect_info`) so the rate limiter can fall back to the peer address.

| Variable | Default | What it is |
|---|---|---|
| `MONGO_URI` | `mongodb://localhost:27017/chatdb` | The database. The name comes from the URI path, else `chatdb` |
| `AGENT_URL` | `http://agent:3000` | Base of `agent`'s `/ask` |
| `HISTORY_MAX_MESSAGES` | `10` | Messages sent as history. Read once at start. `0` or not a number stops the service at start |
| `PORT` | `3000` | HTTP port |

Fixed in code:

| Limit | Value | Applies to |
|---|---|---|
| `MAX_MESSAGES` | 100 | Messages in one conversation |
| `MAX_MESSAGE_LENGTH` | 8000 characters | One question, after trimming |
| `MAX_TITLE_LENGTH` | 120 characters | A title, after trimming |
| `DEFAULT_TITLE` | `New chat` | A conversation created without `title` |
| Agent connect timeout | 5 s | Opening the connection to `agent` |
| Agent read timeout | 60 s | Each wait for the next bytes of the stream |
| Rate limit | 300 per minute | Requests per client IP |

There is no total timeout on the `agent` call: a long answer may take minutes. The read timeout still ends a stream that stops sending.

The API shape is pinned in `schemas/fixtures/chat-conversation.json`. The frontend reads it only through `src/utils/commons/chat.ts`.

---

## Tests

```bash
just test chat               # unit, no infra
just test chat-integration   # tests/*.rs against a throwaway Mongo and a mock agent
```

| Where | What it checks |
|---|---|
| `domain/conversation.rs` | Bare string ids. snake_case citations. `section_path` null. Trim then measure. The cap at 98 and 99. The history window. The title only on the first exchange, cut at 120. The `chat-conversation.json` fixture round-trips and its rejected cases fail |
| `service/conversations.rs` | Tokens then a stored message. `answer` wins, else the tokens. An aborted or unterminated stream stores nothing. An unreachable agent fails before the stream. History and header are forwarded. The cap is `409` before the agent. Empty content fails before any load. Someone else's conversation is `404` |
| `adapters/driven/agent.rs` | Every `agent-stream.json` stream decodes as meant. Tolerated frames are survived. A frame split over two reads. A character split over two reads. Several frames in one read. Keep-alive skipped. A bad frame is an error. An early end gives no `done` |
| `http_api/claims.rs`, `exceptions.rs` | The four `401` cases. URL-safe base64. Each error's status and name. Internal causes are never shown |
| `tests/api.rs` | The same flows over HTTP with a real Mongo: ids, titles, ordering by `updatedAt`, `404` for strangers and bad ids, a streamed answer stored, a cleaned answer stored, nothing stored on a broken stream |
| `tests/architecture_fitness.rs` | Layer imports. `ObjectId` confined. No blanket camelCase. The claims header name |

The service tests use `service/fakes.rs`, in-memory stand-ins for both ports. That is why a streaming exchange can be tested with no Mongo and no `agent`.

---

## Gaps

- **Sends to one conversation are not serialised.** Two questions sent at the same moment each read the same history and both get stored. Only the frontend, which waits for one answer before the next, prevents it.
- **The cap is checked before the answer, not at the write.** Two sends at 98 messages can both pass and leave 102.
- **Rate limiting counts every request.** `/health` and `/metrics` go through the same 300 per minute per IP, and many people behind one address share one counter.
- **A closed tab loses the answer.** Nothing is saved until `done`, and `agent` has already done the work.
